module sh.sysl.gc

// For `trailing_zeros`, which is the bit scan the free-list mask is read with. A trait of the
// language's own numbers rather than a capability: `requires {}` below stays exact.
import sysl.math.Bits
import sysl.raw.ptr_cast

// A tracing garbage collector over storage the caller supplies.
//
// Reference counting cannot free a cycle, and a language with objects that point at each other
// eventually makes one: a JavaScript closure capturing the scope that holds it, a parent holding
// children that hold their parent, an event handler registered on the object it belongs to. `weak`
// is the answer where the back-edge is known at the point it is written, and an interpreter running
// somebody else's program does not have that. So it traces.
//
// **The collector never learns the client's types.** Every object points at a `Kind` describing how
// to trace it, which is the same type erasure sysl's own ARC uses to put a destructor behind a hook:
// one code path, no static type, and a heap that may hold anything.
//
// ## Why this runs on a microcontroller
//
// The hard part of a portable collector is not marking, it is finding the roots. A conservative
// collector -- Boehm's is the one everybody reaches for -- scans the machine stack, the registers
// and the static data segment, and every one of those is a per-target question answered by a linker
// script and a register spill. That is why such a collector is a porting project rather than a
// dependency.
//
// **This one never scans anything.** The client hands over a function that greys its roots, because
// an interpreter already knows where they are: its value stack, its scope chain, its globals. What
// is left needs no operating system, no libc, no threads and no memory mapping -- only a block of
// bytes, which on a hosted machine comes from `malloc` and on a bare one is a static array. That is
// the whole of its portability story, and it is why `requires {}` is exact.
//
// ## What it is not
//
// It is simple on purpose: mark and sweep, stop the world, non-moving. Nothing here is generational
// and nothing has a write barrier.
//
// What it is *not* simple about is the free list, and that is deliberate. A collector's sweep hands
// back tens of thousands of blocks at a stroke, so an allocator that searches one list for a block
// that fits gets slower in proportion to how much the last collection freed -- which is to say it
// gets slower exactly when it has just been given the most to work with. The storage below is
// segregated by size class for that reason; `alloc` says what it costs and why.
//
// **Non-moving is what makes it easy to use, and that is not a consolation prize.** An object never
// changes address, so a client may hold a raw `*T` to a collected object anywhere -- in a local, in
// a struct, across a call -- without a handle table and without the collector being told. A copying
// collector buys locality and pays for it by making every one of those illegal.

// Grey everything this object references, by calling `mark` on each. Every object kind has one.
type Tracer = *extern(*u8, *Heap) -> unit

// Release what this object owns outside the collected heap -- a `string`, a `Buf`, a file
// descriptor, a C allocation. Runs during the sweep that frees the object, and from `dispose`.
//
// **It is handed the heap so that it can reach `context(h)`** -- a runtime whose finalizers give a
// handle back to the runtime needs the runtime, and without the heap the only way to it is a global.
// The heap is for reading the context and nothing else: a finalizer runs while the sweep is walking
// the block, so it may not allocate, may not mark and may not resurrect what it is finalizing.
type Finalizer = *extern(*u8, *Heap) -> unit

// Clear this object's weak slots. Runs after marking is final and before the sweep, on live objects
// only, and its one legal question is `is_live`.
//
// This is what a `WeakRef` is: an object whose tracer does *not* mark its target, plus a `Weaker`
// that nulls the target once marking has decided the target is dead.
type Weaker = *extern(*u8, *Heap) -> unit

// Mark the values whose keys turned out to be live, and answer whether that marked anything new.
//
// **This is what makes a weak map not leak, and it is the one hook whose absence is silent.** A weak
// map holding `key -> value` where the value refers back to its key is the ordinary case --
// `wm.set(node, {owner: node})` -- and a collector that marks values strongly keeps that pair alive
// for as long as the map lives, which is exactly the leak a weak map exists to prevent. Marking
// values weakly instead is worse than the leak: a value nothing else holds would be freed while its
// key is still in use.
//
// The answer is neither. A value is marked *because* its key was marked, which means marking has to
// run again afterwards, because that value may reference a key of some other entry. `collect` loops
// this hook and the worklist together until a pass changes nothing, and that fixpoint is what an
// ephemeron is.
type Ephemeron = *extern(*u8, *Heap) -> bool

// What `collect` is handed: grey every root, by calling `mark` on each.
type Roots = *extern(*Heap) -> unit

// What the collector knows about one kind of object.
//
// **The hooks are here rather than in each object's header because three of the four are usually
// absent.** An interpreter has many object kinds and most need only tracing; four function pointers
// ahead of every allocation would spend three words each on saying "no" three times. A header
// carries one pointer to the descriptor its kind shares, which is the arrangement every real virtual
// machine arrives at and the reason a class pointer exists.
//
// `trace` is required. The other three are `null` where the kind does not need them, which is the
// common case:
//
//     var plain = Kind(&trace_obj, null, null, null)
//
// A `Kind` must outlive every object naming it and its address must not move, so module storage or a
// static is what it is for.
//
// **The layout is public and frozen for 0.3.x: four pointer-sized hooks, in this order.** A client
// may begin its own per-type descriptor with a `Kind` and carry whatever it likes after it -- a
// variant tag, a field count, a name -- and hand `alloc` the address of that descriptor. `kind_of`
// then answers the descriptor itself, and compiled code may load it straight from `p - KIND_BACK`.
struct Kind
    trace: Tracer
    finalize: Finalizer
    weaken: Weaker
    ephemeron: Ephemeron

// How far before a payload its `*Kind` sits: two words, 16 bytes on a 64-bit target and 8 on a
// 32-bit one. **Frozen for 0.3.x**, so that a client may read an object's kind with one load of its
// own -- the `kind_of` below, or inline in generated code.
//
// The word at `p - KIND_BACK + sizeof(usize)` is the collector's, and no client reads it. A client
// that builds an object outside the arena -- a constant in its data segment -- lays out the same
// two words ahead of its payload, a `*Kind` and then a word it leaves zero, and `kind_of` answers
// for it exactly as for a heap object. That kind word is the one header word a client may forge.
const KIND_BACK: usize = 2 * sizeof(usize)

// The header ahead of every object: four words, 32 bytes on a 64-bit target and 16 on a 32-bit one.
//
// `next` links the object list, or a free block into its bin. `gray` is the worklist link rather
// than a second edge in the graph, so the mark phase costs no storage of its own -- an object is on
// the worklist or it is not, and it is threaded through the header it already has.
//
// `word` is the block's length with two flags in its low bits, which a length never uses because
// every block is a multiple of `GRAIN`: `MARKED`, set by `mark` and cleared by the sweep, and `FREE`,
// which says whether this block is storage or an object. `FREE` is what makes the arena walkable end
// to end: every byte the bump pointer has ever handed out belongs to exactly one block whose header
// says how long it is, so `base + 0` and every header after it tile the used part of the arena. That
// is the property the sweep's merge pass reads. One load of `word` answers the length, the mark and
// whether the block is free, which is all the sweep asks of a block.
//
// **There is no "queued" flag, because marking is the queue's guard.** `mark` pushes an object only
// on the edge where `MARKED` goes from clear to set, which happens once per object per collection,
// so no object is ever on the worklist twice.
//
// **`kind` and `word` are the last two words before the payload**, which is what `KIND_BACK` and the
// `@assert` under this struct promise; the rest of the header is private and may be repacked.
private struct Header
    next: *Header
    gray: *Header
    kind: *Kind
    word: usize

@assert(sizeof(Header) - offsetof(Header, kind) == KIND_BACK, "a payload's kind is KIND_BACK before it")
@assert(sizeof(Header) == 4 * sizeof(usize), "the header is four words")

// The flags in a header's `word`, and the mask that leaves its length.
private const MARKED: usize = 1
private const FREE: usize = 2
private const FLAGS: usize = 15

@assert(FLAGS == GRAIN - 1, "the flags sit in the bits a grain-multiple length leaves clear")

private size_of(o: *Header) -> usize = o.word & ~FLAGS
private is_free(o: *Header) -> bool = o.word & FREE != 0

// The four constants below are module-visible rather than file-private because `tests.sysl` holds a
// reference implementation of `bin_of` and checks the two agree at every size. A reference written
// against its own copies of 16, 64, 24 and 1024 would agree with a `bin_of` that had drifted from
// all four, which is not a check of anything.

// Sizes are multiples of this, because `align_up` rounds every block to it.
private[gc] const GRAIN: usize = 16

// One bin per exact size up to `SMALL_BINS * GRAIN`, then one per power of two above it.
//
// **The small classes are exact, which is what makes the common case free of any search at all.** A
// bin holds blocks of one size only, so its head either is the block to hand back or the bin is
// empty -- no block is ever examined and rejected. Sixteen-byte spacing to a kilobyte is 64 bins,
// and an interpreter's objects are all in there: a cons cell, a small string, a closure, an object
// with a handful of properties.
//
// **Above a kilobyte exactness stops being affordable and the classes go geometric**, each bin
// holding a range `[1024 << j, 2048 << j)`. A bin like that has to be searched, so the search is
// bounded -- see `alloc`.
private[gc] const SMALL_BINS: usize = 64
private[gc] const LARGE_BINS: usize = 24
private[gc] const NBINS: usize = 88
private[gc] const LARGE_MIN: usize = 1024

// How many blocks of a large class `alloc` examines before giving up on that class.
private const SCAN: usize = 8

// How many bits a `usize` has, taken from the type rather than written as 64, so that `bin_of` is
// as right on a 32-bit microcontroller as it is on a laptop.
//
// **The obvious portable spelling, `n.count_ones() + n.count_zeros()`, is a trap here.** It is what
// `sysl.slices` uses and it is correct, but it is two population counts of a *runtime* value, and
// arm64 has no scalar popcount -- each one is a round trip through a vector register. Measured on
// the allocator's own benchmark it made `bin_of` slower than the shift loop it replaced. A `sizeof`
// is answered by the compiler and costs nothing.
private const USIZE_BITS: u32 = 8 * u32(sizeof(usize))

// A heap over one contiguous block. Make it with `heap`; everything else here takes a pointer to it.
//
// **The bins are storage of the heap rather than of the module, which is not a style choice.** A
// module-level `var` is filled by an initializer before the program's own statements run, and a
// freestanding target has no loader to run one -- so a package that put its allocator state at
// module scope would be refused by every board build that reached it. Here the caller holds the
// heap, and the heap holds the bins.
struct Heap
    private base: *u8
    private cap: usize
    private ctx: *u8
    private bump: usize
    private all: *Header
    private bins: [NBINS]*Header
    private graylist: *Header
    private live: usize
    private live_bytes: usize
    private steps: usize

    // Which classes have a block on them, one bit per bin, low class in the low bit. `mask_lo`
    // covers bins 0-63 and `mask_hi` the rest.
    //
    // **This is what makes "the next class above this one" a constant-time question.** Without it
    // the search for a larger block is a walk up the array looking for a head that is not null, and
    // that walk is the steady state rather than the exception: a sweep coalesces neighbours into
    // large blocks and a split returns the remainder to a large class, so the exact small class a
    // request wants is usually empty and every allocation reads most of the array. With the bits
    // the same question is a shift and a `trailing_zeros`, which is one instruction on every machine
    // sysl targets.
    //
    // **The invariant is exact and total: bit `b` is set if and only if `bins[b]` is not null.**
    // Every assignment to `bins` maintains it, and a test asserts it after each operation -- a bit
    // set over an empty bin costs a null check and nothing worse, but a bit *clear* over a full one
    // hides storage from the allocator for ever, and the symptom of that is a heap that answers
    // `null` with most of its block free.
    private mask_lo: u64
    private mask_hi: u64

    // How many live objects have a kind carrying a `Weaker` hook, and how many one carrying an
    // `Ephemeron`. Raised in `hand_out`, lowered where the sweep frees one, zeroed by `dispose`.
    //
    // **These are what let `collect` skip its two weak passes, and those passes are most of what a
    // collection costs a program with no weak value in it.** Each is a walk of the whole object list,
    // live and dead, asking every object whether its kind has the hook -- and for a program that
    // made no weak reference and no weak map the answer is no, every time, for every object. At zero
    // the pass is not run at all; at one or more it runs exactly as it always did.
    private weakeners: usize
    private ephemerals: usize

    // How many times a weak pass has walked the object list since the heap was made. It is here for
    // the same reason `steps` is: whether a collection skipped work is a question a test can answer
    // with a count and not with a clock.
    private weak_walks: usize

private align_up(n: usize) -> usize = (n + 15) & ~15usize

// Record that bin `b` has a block on it.
private[gc] bin_set(h: *Heap, b: usize)
    if b < 64
        h.mask_lo = h.mask_lo | 1u64 << u32(b)
    else
        h.mask_hi = h.mask_hi | 1u64 << u32(b - 64)

// Record that bin `b` has none. Called where an unlink leaves a head null, and nowhere else.
private[gc] bin_clear(h: *Heap, b: usize)
    if b < 64
        h.mask_lo = h.mask_lo & ~(1u64 << u32(b))
    else
        h.mask_hi = h.mask_hi & ~(1u64 << u32(b - 64))

// The lowest non-empty class at or above `from`, or `NBINS` if there is none.
//
// The shift is always by less than the width of the word it is applied to -- `from` below 64 shifts
// `mask_lo`, and at or above it shifts `mask_hi` by `from - 64`, which is at most `NBINS - 65`. A
// shift by the whole width is undefined on the machines this compiles to, so the split is not
// merely tidy.
private[gc] first_bin_from(h: *Heap, from: usize) -> usize
    if from >= NBINS then return NBINS

    if from < 64
        val lo = h.mask_lo >> u32(from)

        if lo != 0u64 then return from + usize(lo.trailing_zeros())
        if h.mask_hi != 0u64 then return 64 + usize(h.mask_hi.trailing_zeros())

        return NBINS

    val hi = h.mask_hi >> u32(from - 64)

    if hi != 0u64 then return from + usize(hi.trailing_zeros())

    NBINS
end first_bin_from

// What the mask and the bins each say about one class, and which class a request of `size` payload
// bytes is served from.
//
// **These are module-visible rather than file-private because the invariant above is what the test
// file checks, and it cannot be checked from outside.** `bins` and `Header` stay file-private -- a
// test has no business walking a block -- so the two views of a class are answered here, in scalars,
// and compared there. Nothing in the package's public surface grows.
private[gc] bin_bit(h: *Heap, b: usize) -> bool
    if b < 64 then return h.mask_lo >> u32(b) & 1u64 == 1u64

    h.mask_hi >> u32(b - 64) & 1u64 == 1u64
end bin_bit

private[gc] bin_empty(h: *Heap, b: usize) -> bool = h.bins[b] == null

private[gc] bin_length(h: *Heap, b: usize) -> usize
    var n: usize = 0
    var f = h.bins[b]

    while f != null
        n += 1
        f = f.next

    n
end bin_length

private[gc] class_of_request(size: usize) -> usize = bin_of(align_up(size + sizeof(Header)))

// What one header says about the object at `p` -- its block's length, its mark, whether it is free --
// and the block a request of `size` payload bytes takes, for the tests of the header's packing.
private[gc] block_bytes(p: *u8) -> usize = size_of(header_of(p))
private[gc] block_marked(p: *u8) -> bool = header_of(p).word & MARKED != 0
private[gc] block_free(p: *u8) -> bool = is_free(header_of(p))
private[gc] block_length(size: usize) -> usize = align_up(size + sizeof(Header))
private[gc] arena_room(h: *Heap) -> usize = h.cap

// The smallest block that can hold a header, which is the smallest thing a split may leave behind.
private min_block() -> usize = align_up(sizeof(Header))

// Which bin a block of this size belongs in: its exact class below a kilobyte, its power of two
// above. The last class is open-ended, so every size lands somewhere.
//
// **The large class is a bit scan and not a loop, which is what an allocation is measured in.** The
// obvious way to find which power of two a size falls in is to shift it right until it is one and
// count the shifts, and that is a loop of up to `LARGE_BINS` iterations -- twenty-three shifts and
// twenty-three compares -- run on *every* block that goes on a list. It is easy to believe that
// only a large request pays it, and that is the mistake: a heap in its steady state is one big free
// block, so an ordinary small allocation splits it and pushes the large remainder back, and the
// remainder is what the loop is run on. A sampled profile of an interpreter on this package put
// that loop's `j += 1` as the hottest instruction in `alloc`.
//
// `leading_zeros` answers the same question in one instruction on every machine sysl targets, and
// `USIZE_BITS` reads the width of the word rather than assuming 64 -- so this is as right on a
// 32-bit microcontroller as it is on a laptop. `bin_of` is module-visible rather than file-private
// so that `tests.sysl` can hold the loop above as a reference and assert that the two agree at
// every size, which is the only way a rewrite like this is worth making.
private[gc] bin_of(size: usize) -> usize
    val i = size / GRAIN

    if i < SMALL_BINS then return i

    // At least `SMALL_BINS * GRAIN`, which is `LARGE_MIN`, so `n` is at least one and its top bit
    // exists. `leading_zeros` answers the width for zero, and a width is not a class -- the branch
    // above is what keeps the subtraction meaningful.
    val n = size / LARGE_MIN
    val j = usize(USIZE_BITS - 1 - n.leading_zeros())

    // The last class is open-ended: everything from `LARGE_MIN << (LARGE_BINS - 1)` up lands on it.
    if j >= LARGE_BINS then return NBINS - 1

    SMALL_BINS + j
end bin_of

// Make the `size` bytes at `o` one free block, and put it on the list for its class.
private push_free(h: *Heap, o: *Header, size: usize)
    val b = bin_of(size)

    o.word = size | FREE
    o.next = h.bins[b]
    h.bins[b] = o
    bin_set(h, b)

// Unlink a block of at least `need` bytes from bin `b`, examining at most `limit` of them.
private take_from_bin(h: *Heap, b: usize, need: usize, limit: usize) -> *Header
    var prev: *Header = null
    var f = h.bins[b]
    var seen: usize = 0

    while f != null && seen < limit
        h.steps += 1
        seen += 1

        if size_of(f) >= need
            if prev == null
                h.bins[b] = f.next
                if f.next == null then bin_clear(h, b)
            else
                prev.next = f.next
            return f

        prev = f
        f = f.next

    null
end take_from_bin

// Unlink the head of the first bin above `b` that has one.
//
// **The head is taken without its size being read, which is the point of the arrangement.** Every
// block in a bin above `b` is larger than every block in `b`, so any of them fits a request that
// belongs in `b` -- there is nothing to compare and nothing to reject.
//
// **Which bin that is comes from the mask rather than from a walk, and that is the difference
// between a bounded cost and a cheap-looking one.** The classes above a request are searched on
// *every* allocation whose own class is empty, and after a collection that is nearly all of them:
// the sweep merges neighbours into large blocks and `split` gives remainders back to large classes,
// so a request for a small object finds its exact class bare and starts climbing. Climbing meant a
// dependent load per class, up to 87 of them, for an answer the mask gives in a shift and a count.
private take_larger(h: *Heap, b: usize) -> *Header
    val j = first_bin_from(h, b + 1)

    if j >= NBINS then return null

    val f = h.bins[j]

    h.steps += 1
    h.bins[j] = f.next

    if f.next == null then bin_clear(h, j)

    f
end take_larger

// Keep `need` bytes of `o` and give the remainder back as a block of its own, where there is enough
// of it to be one.
//
// **Without this a class that ran out would eat a larger block whole**, and a heap that allocated a
// kilobyte, freed it, and then wanted a dozen small objects would answer the first one out of the
// kilobyte and be full.
private split(h: *Heap, o: *Header, need: usize)
    val rest = size_of(o) - need

    if rest < min_block() then return

    var t: *Header = ptr_cast(usize(o) + need)

    t.gray = null
    t.kind = null
    o.word = need

    push_free(h, t, rest)
end split

// Link a block into the object list, clear the caller's bytes where it asked for that, and answer
// the payload.
private hand_out(h: *Heap, o: *Header, kind: *Kind, size: usize, clear: bool) -> *u8
    val bytes = size_of(o)

    o.gray = null
    o.kind = kind
    o.word = bytes
    o.next = h.all
    h.all = o
    h.live += 1

    // **The block, not what was asked for.** A block is what it is -- a reused one trimmed to fit, a
    // fresh one rounded up -- and that is what it costs the heap for as long as it is held.
    h.live_bytes += bytes

    if kind.weaken != null then h.weakeners += 1
    if kind.ephemeron != null then h.ephemerals += 1

    val p = payload_of(o)

    if clear then zero(p, size)

    p
end hand_out

// Rebuild every bin from the block itself, merging each run of adjacent free blocks into one.
//
// **Merging is why the bins are rebuilt by walking the block rather than by pushing each block as it
// dies.** Two neighbours that die in the same collection are one block of twice the size, and
// nothing but an address-ordered pass can see that: the object list is in allocation order, and a
// free list is in whatever order things died. Without it a heap of small objects could never satisfy
// a large request again, however much it freed -- which is the failure that looks like a leak and is
// not one.
//
// `sweep` does this same walk with the freeing folded into it; this is what `dispose` uses, where
// everything has already been freed.
private rebuild_free(h: *Heap)
    for b in 0..<NBINS
        h.bins[b] = null

    h.mask_lo = 0u64
    h.mask_hi = 0u64

    var off: usize = 0
    var start: usize = 0
    var run: usize = 0

    while off < h.bump
        var o: *Header = ptr_cast(usize(h.base) + off)
        val sz = size_of(o)

        if is_free(o)
            if run == 0 then start = off
            run += sz
        else
            if run > 0
                close_run(h, start, run)
                run = 0

        off += sz

    if run > 0 then close_run(h, start, run)
end rebuild_free

private close_run(h: *Heap, start: usize, run: usize)
    push_free(h, ptr_cast(usize(h.base) + start), run)

// Clear a fresh payload before the caller sees it.
//
// **This is what makes a reference-counted member safe to store, and it is not a convenience.**
// Assigning a `string`, a `Buf` or a `Map` in sysl *releases the previous occupant* -- so a client
// writing the first one into a payload releases whatever bytes were already there. On a block off
// the free list those are a dead object's, of whatever kind held it last; on a `malloc`ed heap they
// are the allocator's leavings from the very first allocation. Either way the release is applied to
// something that is not a live reference, and the process takes a bus error inside a tracer or a
// finaliser, a long way from the assignment that caused it.
//
// Zeroing costs a write per byte and removes a whole class of failure that has no diagnostic.
private zero(p: *u8, n: usize)
    for i in 0..<n
        p[i] = 0u8

private payload_of(o: *Header) -> *u8 = ptr_cast(usize(o) + sizeof(Header))

private header_of(p: *u8) -> *Header = ptr_cast(usize(p) - sizeof(Header))

// A heap over `cap` bytes at `base`. The block must outlive the heap and must not be touched by
// anything else; a hosted program usually `malloc`s it once at startup, and a freestanding one
// points at a static array.
//
// **Every payload is 16-byte aligned.** Blocks are multiples of `GRAIN` and tile the arena from its
// first byte, so if the first header sits where its payload lands on a 16-byte boundary, every
// header does. The arena is entered `lead` bytes in to make that so, and `lead` is less than 16.
// Those bytes come out of `cap`; an arena no bigger than `lead` holds nothing and refuses every
// allocation. A `cap` within a header and a grain of the whole `usize` range is cut to that, which
// is what lets `take` refuse an oversized request with a single compare and no risk of wrap.
//
// `ctx` is the client's own state -- an interpreter's `Vm`, a runtime's shared tables -- held for the
// hooks to read back with `context`. It is per heap: one heap, one context, whatever thread is
// running. The collector never reads through it, and `null` is a fine value for a client that keeps
// no context or finds its state some other way.
//
// Making a heap is constant work and touches no byte of `base`, however large `cap` is.
heap(base: *u8, cap: usize, ctx: *u8) -> Heap
    val lead = (GRAIN - (usize(base) + sizeof(Header)) % GRAIN) % GRAIN
    val most = ~0usize - sizeof(Header) - (GRAIN - 1)
    val left = if cap > lead then cap - lead else 0
    val room = if left > most then most else left
    Heap(&base[lead], room, ctx, 0, null, [null; NBINS], null, 0, 0, 0, 0u64, 0u64, 0, 0, 0)

// The `ctx` this heap was made with. One load, for a hook that needs the client's state.
@inline
context(h: *Heap) -> *u8 = h.ctx

// The `Kind` an object was allocated with: one load, `KIND_BACK` bytes before the payload.
//
// **It answers whatever address `alloc` was handed**, so a client whose descriptors begin with a
// `Kind` gets its descriptor back and may cast it to its own type. It is valid on every payload of
// this heap's live objects, and on any address the client laid out the same way outside the arena --
// see `KIND_BACK`. The heap is not consulted, so it is not an argument.
@inline
kind_of(p: *u8) -> *Kind
    val w: **Kind = ptr_cast(usize(p) - KIND_BACK)
    w[0]

// Storage for one object of `kind`, zeroed, for the caller to initialize.
//
// **The payload is zeroed, which is what makes a reference-counted member safe to store.** See
// `zero` above for why raw bytes are not merely untidy here but actively unsafe.
//
// **The result is `null` when the heap is full**, and a caller that ignores that will write through
// it. There is no growing: the block handed to `heap` is all there is, which is the property that
// makes the collector usable where there is no allocator to grow with.
//
// **Allocation never collects.** It cannot: an object reached only from a sysl local is invisible to
// a mark phase that traces the client's roots, so a collection triggered from inside an allocation
// would sweep away the object the caller is midway through building. A client collects when it
// chooses, at a point where it knows what is live -- the top of a statement loop, or a bytecode
// dispatch boundary. See the README.
alloc(h: *Heap, size: usize, kind: *Kind) -> *u8 = take(h, size, kind, true)

// Storage for one object of `kind`, **not** zeroed, for a caller that writes every byte of it
// before anything can observe the block -- the collector included.
//
// Everything `alloc` says holds here too: the result is `null` when the heap is full, the block is
// linked into the object list, and allocating never collects.
//
// **What the caller takes on, which is the whole of the difference.** The bytes handed back are
// whatever was last in them: a dead object's fields on a block off a free list, or the host
// allocator's leavings on a block that has never been used. So:
//
//   - **Every byte of `size` is written before the next allocation and before the next safepoint.**
//     A tracer runs against this object the moment a collection starts, and it reads the payload as
//     the object's type says to -- so a half-written payload is a tracer following a pointer that is
//     not one. There is no window here in which "I will fill it in shortly" is safe, because the
//     collector is the other reader and it is not scheduled by the caller.
//   - **No reference-counted member is assigned into it first.** Assigning a `string`, a `Buf` or a
//     `Map` in sysl releases the previous occupant, and the previous occupant is garbage. Such a
//     member must be *initialized* by writing the raw bytes, or the object must come from `alloc`.
//
// **`alloc` is the right entry point unless a measurement says otherwise**, and the measurement has
// to be of the caller rather than of this package: the win exists only where the caller was going to
// overwrite the whole payload anyway, which is an interpreter's constructor and is not much else.
// On this package's own allocate-and-drop benchmark the zeroing is about 30% of the time an
// allocation costs, so the ceiling is high and the applicability is narrow.
alloc_raw(h: *Heap, size: usize, kind: *Kind) -> *u8 = take(h, size, kind, false)

// The body both entry points share. `clear` is a constant at each of the two call sites, so the
// branch in `hand_out` is folded away rather than tested per allocation.
private take(h: *Heap, size: usize, kind: *Kind, clear: bool) -> *u8
    // **A request is validated before anything is touched.** `size + header` and its rounding up to
    // a grain both wrap for a size near the top of the `usize` range, and a wrapped value is a small
    // block that would be handed out against a huge length. A size too large to round is refused
    // here, and so is a block longer than the whole arena, which no free list could hold either.
    //
    // **One compare does both jobs.** `heap` keeps `cap` at or below `~0usize` less a header and a
    // grain's rounding, so a size not above `cap` cannot wrap when the header is added and the sum
    // rounded up, on a word of any width. A request
    // the rounding pushes past `cap` is refused later without a test of its own: every free block is
    // at most `cap` long, so none is big enough, and the bump check below fails for it.
    if size > h.cap then return null

    // A kind with no tracer would be called at the next collection; under `checks` it stops here,
    // at the allocation that named it, instead.
    if option_checks
        if kind == null then trap()
        if kind.trace == null then trap()

    val need = align_up(size + sizeof(Header))

    val want = bin_of(need)

    // **What an allocation costs, which is the whole reason the bins are here.** A request is served
    // from its own class first: a small class is exact, so its head fits without being looked at, and
    // a large class spans a range, so at most `SCAN` of its blocks are examined. Failing that, the
    // head of the first class above it is taken, and every block there is larger than anything this
    // class could hold, so it fits by construction. Either way the number of free blocks examined is
    // bounded by a constant -- it does not grow with how much the last collection freed, which is
    // what a single first-fit list does and why an interpreter running under one slows down as it
    // allocates.
    var limit = SCAN

    if want < SMALL_BINS then limit = 1

    var f = take_from_bin(h, want, need, limit)

    if f == null then f = take_larger(h, want)

    if f != null
        split(h, f, need)
        return hand_out(h, f, kind, size, clear)

    if need <= h.cap - h.bump
        var o: *Header = ptr_cast(usize(h.base) + h.bump)

        h.bump += need
        o.word = need
        return hand_out(h, o, kind, size, clear)

    // **The bounded search is not good enough to answer `null` on**, because a large class holds a
    // range of sizes and a block that fits may lie further down it than the scan reached. There is
    // nothing left to be quick for, so the rest of that class is walked before the heap is declared
    // full. A small class needs no second look: it is exact, and the classes above it were all
    // checked at their heads.
    // `cap` is "as many as there are": a block is at least a header long, so no bin can hold as many
    // blocks as the heap has bytes. Written as a maximum-valued constant instead, this would not fit
    // the `usize` of a 32-bit target -- which is where this package is meant to run.
    f = take_from_bin(h, want, need, h.cap)

    if f == null then return null

    split(h, f, need)
    hand_out(h, f, kind, size, clear)
end take

// Grey one object: a `null` and an object already seen are both ignored, so a tracer may pass
// anything it holds without checking first.
//
// This is the only function a `Tracer` needs, and the only one it may call.
mark(h: *Heap, p: *u8)
    if p == null then return
    if option_checks then check_object(h, p)
    var o = header_of(p)
    val w = o.word
    if w & MARKED != 0 then return
    o.word = w | MARKED
    o.gray = h.graylist
    h.graylist = o

// Whether marking has decided this object survives the collection now running.
//
// **It is meaningful only from inside a `Weaker` or an `Ephemeron`**, which is where marking is
// finished or is between passes. Asked at any other time it reports the previous collection's
// answer, which is `false` for everything, since a sweep clears the marks it leaves behind.
is_live(h: *Heap, p: *u8) -> bool
    if p == null then return false
    if option_checks then check_object(h, p)
    header_of(p).word & MARKED != 0

private drain(h: *Heap)
    while h.graylist != null
        var o = h.graylist
        h.graylist = o.gray
        o.kind.trace(payload_of(o), h)

// What `checks` asks of every pointer handed to `mark`: that it is the payload of a live object of
// this heap. Each failure is a trap, at the mark that was handed the bad pointer rather than at the
// sweep that would otherwise trip over what the mark wrote.
//
// A payload lies between the first header's end and the bump pointer, the far end included -- a
// zero-byte object at the top of the used part has its payload exactly there.
private check_object(h: *Heap, p: *u8)
    val off = usize(p) -% usize(h.base)

    if off < sizeof(Header) || off > h.bump then trap()
    if usize(p) % GRAIN != 0 then trap()

    val o = header_of(p)

    if is_free(o) then trap()
    if o.word & FLAGS & ~(MARKED | FREE) != 0 then trap()
    if size_of(o) < min_block() then trap()

// What `checks` asks of the heap after every sweep and every `dispose`: the blocks tile the used part
// exactly, no two free blocks are neighbours, every free block is on the bin for its size, every bin
// holds only free blocks of its class, and the class mask says which bins have one.
private check_heap(h: *Heap)
    var off: usize = 0
    var free_blocks: usize = 0
    var last_free = false

    while off < h.bump
        val o: *Header = ptr_cast(usize(h.base) + off)

        val sz = size_of(o)

        if sz < min_block() || sz > h.bump - off then trap()
        if o.word & FLAGS & ~FREE != 0 then trap()
        if is_free(o)
            if last_free then trap()
            free_blocks += 1
        last_free = is_free(o)
        off += sz

    if off != h.bump then trap()

    var binned: usize = 0

    for b in 0..<NBINS
        if bin_bit(h, b) != (h.bins[b] != null) then trap()
        var f = h.bins[b]
        while f != null
            if !is_free(f) || bin_of(size_of(f)) != b then trap()
            binned += 1
            f = f.next

    if binned != free_blocks then trap()
end check_heap

// Grey the roots, settle what is reachable, clear dead weak slots, then free what was not reached.
//
// **The drain is a loop rather than a recursion**, so a linked list a million long and a tree a
// million deep both cost O(1) stack. That is not an optimization -- a recursive mark phase is a
// stack overflow waiting for a client's data to get large enough, and it is the one place where
// being simple and being correct come apart.
//
// The four phases, in the order they must run:
//
//   1. **Mark.** The roots are greyed and the worklist drains.
//   2. **Ephemerons.** Every live kind with an `Ephemeron` hook is asked to mark the values whose
//      keys turned out live, and the worklist drains again. That can make another key live, so it
//      repeats until a whole pass changes nothing.
//   3. **Weaken.** Every live kind with a `Weaker` hook clears the slots whose targets did not
//      survive. This is after marking is final and before anything is freed, so a weak slot is never
//      read after its target's storage has gone.
//   4. **Sweep.** Unreached objects are finalized and returned to the free list.
//
// Phases 2 and 3 are each skipped outright where no live object's kind has their hook, which is
// every collection of a program that holds no weak value -- see `Heap.weakeners`.
collect(h: *Heap, roots: Roots)
    roots(h)
    drain(h)

    var changed = h.ephemerals > 0
    while changed
        changed = false
        h.weak_walks += 1
        var e = h.all
        while e != null
            if e.word & MARKED != 0 && e.kind.ephemeron != null
                if e.kind.ephemeron(payload_of(e), h)
                    changed = true
            e = e.next
        if changed then drain(h)

    if h.weakeners > 0
        h.weak_walks += 1
        var w = h.all
        while w != null
            if w.word & MARKED != 0 && w.kind.weaken != null
                w.kind.weaken(payload_of(w), h)
            w = w.next

    sweep(h)
end collect

// Free every object marking did not reach, rebuild the object list from the survivors, and rebuild
// every bin with neighbouring free blocks merged -- all in ONE walk of the block, in address order.
//
// **This used to be two walks, and the first was the expensive one.** The sweep followed the object
// list, which is in allocation order and, once storage is being reused, in no order the memory
// system can predict: every step was a dependent load from wherever the next object happened to be.
// Then `rebuild_free` walked the block from `base` to the bump pointer anyway, because merging
// neighbours needs address order. That second walk already visits every object's header, so it can
// do the sweep's work as it passes -- and the object list, which nothing reads for its order, is
// relinked from the survivors in the order they are met. What is left per block is one sequential
// read of a header that the next one sits directly after.
//
// Per dead object that is a size, a mark bit, a kind pointer and the kind's `finalize` -- and a
// call only where that is not null. The weak counts are read off the kind only while a weak object
// is live at all, which is `Heap.weakeners`'s reason again.
private sweep(h: *Heap)
    for b in 0..<NBINS
        h.bins[b] = null

    h.mask_lo = 0u64
    h.mask_hi = 0u64

    val counted = h.weakeners > 0 || h.ephemerals > 0
    var tail: *Header = null
    var off: usize = 0
    var start: usize = 0
    var run: usize = 0

    h.all = null

    while off < h.bump
        var o: *Header = ptr_cast(usize(h.base) + off)
        val w = o.word
        val sz = w & ~FLAGS

        if w & (MARKED | FREE) == MARKED
            o.word = sz
            if tail == null then h.all = o else tail.next = o
            tail = o
            if run > 0
                close_run(h, start, run)
                run = 0
        else
            if w & FREE == 0
                val k = o.kind
                if k.finalize != null then k.finalize(payload_of(o), h)
                if counted
                    if k.weaken != null then h.weakeners -= 1
                    if k.ephemeron != null then h.ephemerals -= 1
                o.word = sz | FREE
                h.live -= 1
                h.live_bytes -= sz
            if run == 0 then start = off
            run += sz

        off += sz

    if tail != null then tail.next = null
    if run > 0 then close_run(h, start, run)
    if option_checks then check_heap(h)
end sweep

// Finalize and free every object, live or not, leaving an empty heap over the same block.
//
// **This is what runs at the end of a program**, and without it a finalizer that closes a file or
// releases a `Buf` never runs for anything still reachable when the interpreter stops. Nothing is
// traced and nothing is marked: teardown has no reachability question to answer.
dispose(h: *Heap)
    var p = h.all
    while p != null
        val nxt = p.next
        if p.kind.finalize != null
            p.kind.finalize(payload_of(p), h)
        p.word = size_of(p) | FREE
        p = nxt
    h.all = null
    h.graylist = null
    h.live = 0
    h.live_bytes = 0
    h.weakeners = 0
    h.ephemerals = 0
    rebuild_free(h)
    if option_checks then check_heap(h)

// How many objects are live as of the last sweep.
live(h: *Heap) -> usize = h.live

// How many live objects have a kind with a `Weaker` hook, and how many one with an `Ephemeron`.
// `collect` runs each weak pass only where its count is above zero.
weak_objects(h: *Heap) -> usize = h.weakeners
ephemeron_objects(h: *Heap) -> usize = h.ephemerals

// How many times a weak pass has walked the object list since the heap was made -- one per round of
// the ephemeron fixpoint, one per weaken pass. A collection of a heap holding no weak object adds
// nothing to it.
weak_walks(h: *Heap) -> usize = h.weak_walks

// How many BYTES those objects hold, headers included.
//
// **This is the number a client schedules collections by, and `used` is not.** A heap's limit is
// bytes rather than objects -- a thousand large arrays and a thousand small strings cost wildly
// different amounts of the block -- so a client deciding *when* to collect wants this one. It rises
// with every allocation and falls with every sweep, which is exactly what a threshold needs.
//
// The alternative was tried and is worth naming, because the failure has no symptom but slowness:
// a client that thresholds on `used` asks "has the heap EVER grown past N", which is true forever
// once it is true -- so it runs a full mark-sweep at every allocation point from then on, reclaiming
// almost nothing each time. slate did exactly that and ran 19,629 collections over a 20,000-iteration
// loop.
live_size(h: *Heap) -> usize = h.live_bytes

// How much of the block has been handed out at least once. It never falls: a sweep returns storage
// to the free list rather than to the block, so this is the high-water mark and not the live total.
//
// **It is a diagnostic, not a scheduling input** -- see `live_size` for the one to threshold on.
used(h: *Heap) -> usize = h.bump

// How many free blocks the allocator has looked at since the heap was made.
//
// **This is the allocator's cost in the one unit that matters, and it is here so that a test can
// assert on it.** Whether an allocation is cheap is not a question about wall-clock time on the
// machine the suite happens to run on -- it is a question about how many free blocks have to be
// examined before one is handed back, and that number is either bounded by a constant or it is
// proportional to how much the heap has freed. A first-fit walk over one list is the second kind and
// reads as a performance mystery in the client; a counter makes it a failing assertion here instead.
alloc_steps(h: *Heap) -> usize = h.steps
